\section{The effects table}{

A result that estimates the contrasts of the Solomon design holds them in
\code{effects}, a data frame with one row for each contrast. These functions
return one:
\itemize{
\item \code{\link[=fit_solomon_glm]{fit_solomon_glm()}}, for four-group designs (class \code{solomon_glm}) and
for designs with several treatments (class \code{solomon_ngroup});
\item \code{\link[=fit_solomon_ml]{fit_solomon_ml()}}, \code{\link[=fit_solomon_mi]{fit_solomon_mi()}}, and \code{\link[=fit_solomon_mmrm]{fit_solomon_mmrm()}};
\item \code{\link[=fit_solomon_sem]{fit_solomon_sem()}} and \code{\link[=fit_solomon_sem_latent]{fit_solomon_sem_latent()}};
\item \code{\link[=marginal_solomon]{marginal_solomon()}};
\item \code{\link[=solomon_from_summary]{solomon_from_summary()}}, for four-group designs (class
\code{solomon_summary_fit}) and for designs with several treatments (class
\code{solomon_summary_ngroup});
\item \code{\link[=fit_solomon_classic]{fit_solomon_classic()}}, for the historical Tests A-H.
}

Two results hold a table of the same form under the name \code{results},
because its rows come from several fits: \code{\link[=compare_solomon_methods]{compare_solomon_methods()}}, with
one row for each method and contrast, and \code{\link[=tipping_point_solomon]{tipping_point_solomon()}}, with
one row for each offset.

The columns come in three parts, in this order.

\strong{1. The columns that index the rows}, in the tables that need them:
\itemize{
\item \code{comparison}, for designs with several treatments: the conditions
compared, such as \code{"RP vs Control"}. In the rows of the pretest effects
it is the condition, or \code{"All conditions"} for the pretest main effect.
\item \code{occasion}, in \code{\link[=fit_solomon_mmrm]{fit_solomon_mmrm()}}: the posttest occasion, or the two
occasions of a change, such as \code{"3 vs 1"}.
\item \code{test}, in \code{\link[=fit_solomon_classic]{fit_solomon_classic()}} and in \code{\link[=solomon_from_summary]{solomon_from_summary()}} for
four-group designs: the letter of the historical test, and \code{""} for the
pretest main effect, which has none.
\item \code{scale}, in \code{\link[=marginal_solomon]{marginal_solomon()}}: \code{"Risk difference"}, \code{"Risk ratio"},
\code{"Odds ratio"}, \code{"Rate difference"}, or \code{"Rate ratio"}.
\item \code{method}, in \code{\link[=compare_solomon_methods]{compare_solomon_methods()}}: the analysis.
\item \code{delta} and \code{delta_sd}, in \code{\link[=tipping_point_solomon]{tipping_point_solomon()}}: the offset, in
posttest units and in standard deviations.
}

\strong{2. The eight shared columns}, in every table:
\itemize{
\item \code{contrast}: the label of the contrast (see "Contrast labels").
\item \code{estimate}: the estimate, on the scale of the analysis. In most
analyses that is the unit of the outcome. It is the scale of the link,
such as a log odds ratio or a log rate ratio, for the binomial,
Poisson, and negative-binomial models of \code{\link[=fit_solomon_glm]{fit_solomon_glm()}}; the unit
of the latent variable in \code{\link[=fit_solomon_sem_latent]{fit_solomon_sem_latent()}}; and, in
\code{\link[=marginal_solomon]{marginal_solomon()}}, the difference or ratio that \code{scale} names.
\item \code{std.error}: the standard error of the estimate. In
\code{\link[=marginal_solomon]{marginal_solomon()}}, the standard error of a ratio is that of its
logarithm, on which the ratio is tested.
\item \code{statistic}: the statistic of the test that the contrast is zero,
\code{estimate / std.error} (for a ratio in \code{\link[=marginal_solomon]{marginal_solomon()}},
\code{log(estimate) / std.error}).
\item \code{df}: the degrees of freedom of the t distribution to which \code{statistic}
is referred. It is \code{Inf} when the reference distribution is the normal
(a z test), as in the SEM fits, and is never \code{NA}.
\item \code{p.value}: the two-sided p-value, \code{2 * pt(-abs(statistic), df)}. It is
not adjusted for the number of contrasts.
\item \code{conf.low} and \code{conf.high}: the limits of the confidence interval at
\code{conf_level}, also unadjusted.
}

\strong{3. The columns of one class}, after \code{conf.high}:
\itemize{
\item \code{r2}, \code{r2_lo}, and \code{r2_hi}, in \code{\link[=fit_solomon_glm]{fit_solomon_glm()}}: the Wald partial
R-squared and its interval.
\item \code{p.adjusted}, for designs with several treatments: the p-value adjusted
across the comparisons.
\item \code{fmi} and \code{mc_se}, in \code{\link[=fit_solomon_mi]{fit_solomon_mi()}}: the fraction of missing
information and the Monte Carlo standard error.
\item \code{F}, in \code{\link[=fit_solomon_classic]{fit_solomon_classic()}} and, for four-group designs, in
\code{\link[=solomon_from_summary]{solomon_from_summary()}}: the square of the t statistic. The latter
adds \code{sumsq}, the Type III sum of squares.
\item \code{adjustment}, \code{variance}, and \code{reference}, in
\code{\link[=compare_solomon_methods]{compare_solomon_methods()}}: the description of each method.
\item \code{significant}, in \code{\link[=tipping_point_solomon]{tipping_point_solomon()}}: whether the p-value is
below \code{alpha}.
}

Read the rows by their labels and the columns by their names. A later
version can add a row, or a column of one class after \code{conf.high}; it
does not rename, reorder, or remove the columns above.
}

\section{Contrast labels}{

The \code{contrast} column takes its values from one set of labels. The four
treatment contrasts compare treated with control participants:
\itemize{
\item \verb{ATE (avg over pretest)}: the average treatment effect, the
equal-weighted average of the treatment effect among pretested
participants and among unpretested participants;
\item \verb{Pretest x Treatment}: the treatment effect among pretested
participants minus that among unpretested participants (pretest
sensitization);
\item \code{Treatment | pretested}: the treatment effect among pretested
participants;
\item \code{Treatment | unpretested}: the treatment effect among unpretested
participants.
}

The three pretest effects compare pretested with unpretested
participants:
\itemize{
\item \verb{Pretest effect | control}: among control participants;
\item \verb{Pretest effect | treated}: among treated participants;
\item \verb{Pretest main effect}: the equal-weighted average of the two (over
all the conditions, for a design with several treatments).
}

One class adds a label: \code{\link[=fit_solomon_mmrm]{fit_solomon_mmrm()}} ends its table with
\verb{Change in Pretest x Treatment}, the Pretest x Treatment contrast at the
last occasion minus that at the first.

The tables differ in the rows they have, not in what a label means:
\itemize{
\item all seven: \code{\link[=fit_solomon_glm]{fit_solomon_glm()}}, \code{\link[=fit_solomon_ml]{fit_solomon_ml()}}, the four-group
models of \code{\link[=fit_solomon_sem]{fit_solomon_sem()}} and \code{\link[=fit_solomon_sem_latent]{fit_solomon_sem_latent()}},
\code{\link[=marginal_solomon]{marginal_solomon()}} on each scale, and \code{\link[=fit_solomon_mmrm]{fit_solomon_mmrm()}} at each
occasion;
\item the four treatment contrasts: \code{\link[=fit_solomon_mi]{fit_solomon_mi()}},
\code{\link[=solomon_from_summary]{solomon_from_summary()}} for designs with several treatments,
\code{\link[=marginal_solomon]{marginal_solomon()}} with \code{method = "cluster_summary"}, and
\code{\link[=compare_solomon_methods]{compare_solomon_methods()}};
\item the four treatment contrasts and \verb{Pretest main effect}:
\code{\link[=solomon_from_summary]{solomon_from_summary()}} for four-group designs;
\item one row for each of Tests A-H, then \verb{Pretest main effect}:
\code{\link[=fit_solomon_classic]{fit_solomon_classic()}}, in which \code{test} tells apart the tests that
estimate the same contrast (\code{Treatment | pretested} is Tests B, E, F,
and G; \code{Treatment | unpretested} is Tests C and H);
\item \code{Treatment | pretested} alone: the ANCOVA models of the pretested
groups in \code{\link[=fit_solomon_sem]{fit_solomon_sem()}} and in \code{effects_pre} of
\code{\link[=fit_solomon_sem_latent]{fit_solomon_sem_latent()}};
\item the one contrast chosen: \code{\link[=tipping_point_solomon]{tipping_point_solomon()}}.
}

Tables of other kinds name the same quantities the same way: the
interaction in an analysis-of-variance table is \verb{Pretest x Treatment}
(\verb{Pretest x Condition} for several treatments), and
\code{\link[=equivalence_solomon]{equivalence_solomon()}}, \code{\link[=perm_solomon]{perm_solomon()}}, \code{\link[=power_solomon]{power_solomon()}},
\code{\link[=plan_solomon]{plan_solomon()}}, and \code{\link[=simulate_solomon]{simulate_solomon()}} name their contrasts with
these labels.
}

\section{Confidence level}{

Every result with an effects table, including the two that name it
\code{results}, has \code{conf_level} at its top level: the confidence level of
\code{conf.low} and \code{conf.high}.
}

\section{tidy()}{

\code{tidy(x)} returns the effects table of \code{x} as it is stored, a data frame,
so \code{identical(tidy(fit), fit$effects)} is \code{TRUE}. For
\code{\link[=compare_solomon_methods]{compare_solomon_methods()}} and \code{\link[=tipping_point_solomon]{tipping_point_solomon()}} it returns
\code{results}. For \code{\link[=fit_solomon_sem_latent]{fit_solomon_sem_latent()}} it returns \code{effects}, the
contrasts of the four-group model; the latent ANCOVA of the pretested
groups is in \code{effects_pre}. solomonR re-exports the generic from the
generics package, so \code{tidy()} works with solomonR alone, and with broom
attached it is the same generic. A result with no effects table has no
method, and \code{tidy()} reports that.
}

\section{Outside the contract}{

\strong{Tables of other kinds.} A table that is not a table of Solomon
contrasts cannot have the columns above, and does not. Each is described
on the help page of its function, and a change to one is reported in the
package news:
\itemize{
\item Tables of coefficients (\code{coefficients} in \code{\link[=fit_solomon_glm]{fit_solomon_glm()}} and
\code{\link[=fit_solomon_ml]{fit_solomon_ml()}}) have the eight shared columns, with \code{term} in place
of \code{contrast}.
\item Analysis-of-variance tables (\code{anova} in \code{\link[=solomon_from_summary]{solomon_from_summary()}} and
\code{\link[=fit_solomon_classic]{fit_solomon_classic()}}) have \code{source}, \code{sumsq}, \code{df}, \code{meansq}, \code{F},
and \code{p.value}.
\item Tables of omnibus tests (\code{omnibus} in \code{\link[=fit_solomon_glm]{fit_solomon_glm()}} for several
treatments) have \code{test}, \code{statistic}, \code{df1}, \code{df2}, \code{p.value}, and
\code{reference}, because such a test has several numerator degrees of
freedom and no single estimate.
\item \code{\link[=baseline_solomon]{baseline_solomon()}} compares pretests, not posttests: its estimate is
\code{difference}, followed by \code{std.error}, \code{statistic}, \code{df}, \code{p.value},
\code{conf.low}, and \code{conf.high}, and it has no \code{contrast}.
\item Test I of \code{\link[=fit_solomon_classic]{fit_solomon_classic()}} combines p-values and estimates no
contrast, and the \code{history} comparisons of that function have no
standard error.
\item \code{\link[=fit_solomon_steyn]{fit_solomon_steyn()}} reproduces a published sequence of t, F,
chi-square, and z tests, in tables with \code{reference}, \code{df1}, and \code{df2}.
Its elements are named for the steps of the sequence, and its \code{effects}
element is the last step, a list of tables, not an effects table. The
\code{\link[=fit_solomon_classic]{fit_solomon_classic()}} results it holds follow the contract.
\item \code{\link[=invariance_solomon]{invariance_solomon()}}, \code{\link[=fisher_solomon]{fisher_solomon()}}, \code{\link[=solomon_effect_sizes]{solomon_effect_sizes()}},
\code{\link[=check_solomon_assumptions]{check_solomon_assumptions()}}, \code{\link[=check_solomon_missing]{check_solomon_missing()}}, and
\code{\link[=validate_solomon]{validate_solomon()}} return model comparisons, tests of two-by-two
tables, effect sizes in the \code{yi} and \code{vi} form of meta-analysis, and
checks.
\item \code{\link[=power_solomon]{power_solomon()}} and \code{\link[=plan_solomon]{plan_solomon()}} describe a planned study, which
has true effects and no estimates: their column of contrast labels is
\code{estimand}, and \code{\link[=power_solomon]{power_solomon()}} adds the label \code{Treatment (one-sided)}
for Test I. The \code{"truth"} attribute of \code{\link[=simulate_solomon]{simulate_solomon()}} names that
column \code{estimand} for a four-group design and \code{contrast} for a design
with several treatments.
\item The \code{table} of \code{\link[=report_solomon]{report_solomon()}} holds the values that the sentences
of the report give. It is the effects table of the fit for
\code{\link[=fit_solomon_glm]{fit_solomon_glm()}}, \code{\link[=fit_solomon_ml]{fit_solomon_ml()}}, \code{\link[=fit_solomon_mi]{fit_solomon_mi()}},
\code{\link[=fit_solomon_mmrm]{fit_solomon_mmrm()}}, \code{\link[=fit_solomon_sem]{fit_solomon_sem()}}, \code{\link[=fit_solomon_sem_latent]{fit_solomon_sem_latent()}},
\code{\link[=marginal_solomon]{marginal_solomon()}}, and \code{\link[=solomon_from_summary]{solomon_from_summary()}} for designs with
several treatments; \code{results} for \code{\link[=tipping_point_solomon]{tipping_point_solomon()}}; and, for
\code{\link[=fit_solomon_classic]{fit_solomon_classic()}}, the rows of \code{effects} for the tests on the
path. For the other results it is a table of another kind: \code{anova} for
a four-group \code{\link[=solomon_from_summary]{solomon_from_summary()}} result; \code{contrast}, \code{estimate},
and the randomization p-value, named \code{p.value}, for \code{\link[=perm_solomon]{perm_solomon()}};
and \code{contrast}, \code{estimate}, \code{p_equivalence}, and \code{outcome} for
\code{\link[=equivalence_solomon]{equivalence_solomon()}}, after \code{comparison} for a design with several
treatments.
}

\strong{Results for one contrast.} \code{\link[=equivalence_solomon]{equivalence_solomon()}} tests one contrast
against two bounds, with two one-sided tests, a test against zero, and
two intervals, so it has no effects table and no \code{tidy()} method. It
returns the values under the names of the columns: \code{contrast},
\code{estimate}, \code{std.error}, \code{statistic} (of the test against zero), \code{df},
\code{conf.low}, and \code{conf.high}, with \code{conf_level}, the level of that
interval (1 - 2 \code{alpha}). Its p-values have their own names
(\code{p_equivalence}, \code{p_lower}, \code{p_upper}, and \code{p_zero}), and there is no
\code{p.value}. \code{\link[=perm_solomon]{perm_solomon()}} returns \code{contrast}, \code{estimate}, and the
randomization p-value \code{p_perm}; its \code{statistic} names the statistic
permuted. \code{\link[=fit_solomon_1949]{fit_solomon_1949()}} returns Solomon's (1949) point estimates.

\strong{Not stable.} These parts of a result can change in any version:
\itemize{
\item Fitted model objects: \code{model} in \code{\link[=fit_solomon_glm]{fit_solomon_glm()}} and
\code{\link[=fit_solomon_mmrm]{fit_solomon_mmrm()}}; \code{fit} and \code{fit_pre}, the lavaan objects of the SEM
fits; the \code{fits} of \code{\link[=invariance_solomon]{invariance_solomon()}}; and the \code{model} of each test
in \code{\link[=fit_solomon_classic]{fit_solomon_classic()}}. What they contain is decided by the package
that made them.
\item \code{settings}, \code{call}, \code{data}, and the other elements that a help page
describes as used by solomonR's own functions.
\item Legacy elements: \code{ancova}, \code{t_unpretested}, and \code{stouffer} in
\code{\link[=fit_solomon_classic]{fit_solomon_classic()}}, which repeat Tests E, H, and I.
\item The one-row summary that \code{generics::glance()} gives for a four-group
\code{\link[=fit_solomon_glm]{fit_solomon_glm()}} fit.
}

\strong{Former names.} These element names were replaced in solomonR 1.0.0.
Each still works with \code{$} through v1.x, with a deprecation warning, but
not with \code{[[}:
\itemize{
\item \code{effects_post} and \code{fit_post} in \code{\link[=fit_solomon_sem_latent]{fit_solomon_sem_latent()}}, now
\code{effects} and \code{fit};
\item \code{contrasts} in \code{\link[=solomon_from_summary]{solomon_from_summary()}}, now \code{effects};
\item \code{aov} in \code{\link[=fit_solomon_classic]{fit_solomon_classic()}}, now \code{anova}, which has Type III sums
of squares.
}

A result saved by an earlier version gives its elements under the new
names as they were stored. Its tables can differ from the contract, and
nothing translates them. A SEM fit saved by an earlier version has the
former contrast labels (\code{ATE}, \code{Sens}, \code{Pre_Eff}, and \code{Unpre_Eff}) and no
\code{df}: \code{tidy()}, \code{\link[=report_solomon]{report_solomon()}}, and \code{\link[=plot_solomon_effects]{plot_solomon_effects()}} stop and
ask for it to be fitted again. So does \code{\link[=report_solomon]{report_solomon()}} for a
four-group \code{\link[=solomon_from_summary]{solomon_from_summary()}} result saved by one, and \code{tidy()} for
a \code{\link[=fit_solomon_classic]{fit_solomon_classic()}} fit saved by one, which has no \code{effects} table.
}

\examples{
fit <- fit_solomon_glm(y_post, treat, pretested, y_pre, data = solomon_example)
tidy(fit)
identical(tidy(fit), fit$effects)
fit$conf_level

# Select rows by label and columns by name.
effects <- tidy(fit)
effects[effects$contrast == "Pretest x Treatment",
        c("estimate", "conf.low", "conf.high")]

# Another analysis has the same labels and the same eight columns.
ml <- fit_solomon_ml(y_post, treat, pretested, y_pre, data = solomon_example)
tidy(ml)$contrast
names(tidy(ml))
}
\references{
Solomon, R. L. (1949). An extension of control group design. \emph{Psychological
Bulletin, 46}(2), 137–150. https://doi.org/10.1037/h0062958
}
\seealso{
\code{\link[=report_solomon]{report_solomon()}}, which writes the contrasts as sentences, and
\code{\link[=plot_solomon_effects]{plot_solomon_effects()}}, which draws them.
}
